  {
   "id": "reverse-surge",
   "title": "Reverse-voltage and surge protection",
   "page": 10,
   "description": "The input side is a voltage source VIN through RIN into IIN, its low side on IRET; VREG and VREF are left free. Between V+ and IO sit a 0.01 µF capacitor and a 36 V Zener D1, cathode on V+. A bridge of four 1N4148 diodes joins the transmitter to the two-wire loop: its positive corner feeds V+, its negative corner takes IO, and either loop wire may land on either AC corner, so a reversed loop still works, at a cost of about 1.4 V of loop voltage. The loop holds the supply VPS and the load RL with their common point grounded.",
