 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Output current",
   "expr": "IO = 100 × IIN",
   "where": {
    "IO": "loop current",
    "IIN": "current into pin 2"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 11
  },
  {
   "name": "Voltage input",
   "expr": "IO = 100 × VIN / RIN",
   "where": {
    "VIN": "input voltage, referred to IRET",
    "RIN": "external input resistor into IIN"
   },
   "note": "IIN sits at the IRET potential.",
   "page": 1
  },
  {
   "name": "Receiver voltage",
   "expr": "VLOAD = RLOAD × IO = RLOAD × IIN × 100 = RLOAD × VSENSOR / RIN × 100",
   "where": {
    "VLOAD": "voltage the receiver reads",
    "RLOAD": "receiver's sense resistor, plus wire resistance on long runs",
    "VSENSOR": "sensor voltage driving RIN"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Start of output rise",
   "expr": "IIN > IQ / 100",
   "where": {
    "IQ": "quiescent current"
   },
   "note": "Below this input the loop carries only IQ plus any VREF and VREG current.",
   "page": 12
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "How it works",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A two-wire transmitter: an internal amplifier A1 forces the current into IIN, times 100, to flow out of IO. Part of the loop current is the chip's own supply current into V+; the rest flows through the external transistor Q1. IIN is held at the IRET potential, so a voltage input needs a series resistor. The XTR115 and XTR116 differ only in the VREF value, and VREF is not used inside the chip."
  },
  {
   "topic": "IRET",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "IRET is the input circuit's local ground. Current taken from VREG and VREF must come back through IRET, so it lands inside the measured loop current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input range",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Full-scale inputs from 1 V up are common; keeping full scale above 0.5 V holds down the effect of A1's offset and drift. With RIN = 20 kΩ, 0.8 V to 4 V gives 40 µA to 200 µA and 4 mA to 20 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Minimum scale current",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "With zero input the loop carries the quiescent current, about 200 µA, and the output does not rise until IIN passes IQ / 100. Any load on VREF or VREG raises that floor; over 3.7 mA is left for external circuits with the loop still able to go below 4 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Low-scale offset",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The 4 mA low scale needs 40 µA into IIN: a resistor from VREF to IIN, or an offset built into the circuit that drives the input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "External transistor",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Q1 carries most of the full-scale current and can dissipate close to 0.8 W at 40 V and 20 mA. Keeping that heat off the die is why the transistor is external; place Q1 away from the XTR11x and other sensitive analog parts, and mount it so its heat leaves through the housing. Almost any NPN rated for the loop's voltage, current and dissipation works; a MOSFET brings no accuracy gain and is not recommended. The chip can run without Q1, but self-heating makes that impractical at higher loop voltage and current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output current limit",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The output is linear to 25 mA and limited near 32 mA. A resistor from pin 3 (IRET) to pin 5 (E) moves the limit to extend the range. All output current passes through internal resistors: more than 45 mA can damage the part permanently."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse-voltage protection",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "With only 7.5 V of compliance needed, protection parts can be placed in the loop path. A diode bridge lets the loop wires be swapped and costs two diode drops, about 1.4 V, leaving a compliance near 9 V. A single series diode from the loop supply to V+ guards against reversal at a cost of about 0.7 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge protection",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Clamp surges on remote loop wiring to as low a voltage as practical with a Zener or surge-clamp diode of the lowest workable rating; 36 V suits normal loop voltages. In characterisation, several production lots took loop supplies up to 65 V without damage. A surge Zener conducts heavily when forward biased, which can harm the receiver if the loop is reversed, so pair it with a series diode or a diode bridge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Modes",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "A single operating mode, inside the recommended operating range."
  },
  {
   "topic": "RF interference",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Long loop wires pick up RF, which the input stage of the XTR11x or the circuit ahead of it can rectify; it shows as an output current that shifts as the loop or input wiring moves. RF can also come in at the input pins; in a compact transmitter with short sensor leads it most often enters through the loop wiring."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Loop stability",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Check the loop's stability in every design. 10 nF from V+ to IO suits most uses; it acts in parallel with RLOAD and forms a pole that limits bandwidth, so HART designs use 2 nF to 3 nF instead. Where EMI and EMC matter, the capacitor needs low ESR to keep VLOOP ripple out of the loop current, where it would show as noise across RLOAD."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitive load on VREF",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The VREF buffer needs compensation when it drives capacitance: both CHF (10 pF to 0.5 µF) and CLF (2.2 µF to 22 µF) are needed, with either a 10 Ω series RISO or a 50 Ω RCOMP in series with CLF. Load current up to 2.5 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Loop supply 7.5 V to 36 V over -40 °C to 85 °C; above 40 V the part can be destroyed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Bypass with 0.1 µF low-ESR ceramics from the supply pin to ground as close to the chip as possible. On a noisy loop supply, filter with decoupling capacitors plus small series resistors or damping inductors in the V+ line."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Chip site origin",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The die comes from two fabrication flows, CSO: SHE and CSO: TID, which differ in some figures (bias current, slew rate, VREF and VREG accuracy, quiescent current); a robust design meets both. The /2K5 reel part numbers may carry either die; XTR115U, XTR116U and XTR116UA carry SHE die."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grades",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The U grade has tighter maximum limits than UA on span error, nonlinearity, offset voltage and its drift, VREF accuracy and VREF drift; typical figures are the same."
  }
 ],
